Create PostgreSQL's child processes with posix_spawn().

This is what makes a real postmaster possible on Unikraft. Together with
building `-DEXEC_BACKEND` (see the Dockerfile) it removes the last bare fork()
from the server: with EXEC_BACKEND every child -- the checkpointer, the
background writer, the WAL writer, the syslogger, autovacuum and one backend
per connection -- is created by `internal_forkexec()`, which forks and then
immediately execs. That is precisely the pattern Unikraft supports, and
posix_spawn() is the safe way to express it. See lib/posix-process/README.md
upstream:

  Being a single-address-space operating system, instead of the traditional
  fork() that creates a new address space for the child, Unikraft implements
  multiprocess with vfork() only. [...] Fortunately it is common that
  applications spawn new processes by calling fork() immediately followed by an
  execve() [...] Applications can use the posix_spawn() libc function that
  spawns a process using vfork() in a safe way.

--- a/src/backend/postmaster/postmaster.c
+++ b/src/backend/postmaster/postmaster.c
@@ -70,6 +70,7 @@
 #include <time.h>
 #include <sys/wait.h>
 #include <ctype.h>
+#include <spawn.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <sys/socket.h>
 #include <fcntl.h>
@@ -4587,20 +4588,43 @@
 	/* Insert temp file name after --fork argument */
 	argv[2] = tmpfilename;
 
-	/* Fire off execv in child */
-	if ((pid = fork_process()) == 0)
+	/*
+	 * Spawn the child with posix_spawn() rather than fork() + execv().
+	 *
+	 * This is the same fork-then-immediately-exec sequence, expressed as the
+	 * one call a single-address-space kernel can implement.  Unikraft has no
+	 * fork(): it creates processes with vfork() only (CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK),
+	 * where the child shares the parent's address space and the parent is
+	 * suspended until the child execs or exits.  Hand-writing that is
+	 * undefined behaviour -- between vfork() and execve() the child may touch
+	 * nothing but the exec, and fork_process() assigns to globals
+	 * (MyStartTime, the stack base, the PRNG seed) that would land in the
+	 * *parent's* memory.  posix_spawn() is exactly this sequence done safely:
+	 * musl implements it with clone(CLONE_VM | CLONE_VFORK) and does the exec
+	 * on its own stack.
+	 *
+	 * Nothing is lost by skipping fork_process(): everything it sets up in the
+	 * child is redone by main() after the exec, which is the whole point of an
+	 * EXEC_BACKEND build.
+	 */
 	{
-		if (execv(postgres_exec_path, argv) < 0)
+		int			rc;
+
+		/* fork_process() flushed these; posix_spawn() will not. */
+		fflush(NULL);
+
+		rc = posix_spawn(&pid, postgres_exec_path, NULL, NULL, argv, environ);
+		if (rc != 0)
 		{
+			errno = rc;
 			ereport(LOG,
-					(errmsg("could not execute server process \"%s\": %m",
+					(errmsg("could not spawn server process \"%s\": %m",
 							postgres_exec_path)));
-			/* We're already in the child process here, can't return */
-			exit(1);
+			return -1;
 		}
 	}
 
-	return pid;					/* Parent returns pid, or -1 on fork failure */
+	return pid;					/* Parent returns pid, or -1 on failure */
 }
 #else							/* WIN32 */
 
